<a class="article-connected-content" href="https://cacm.acm.org/research-highlights/technical-perspective-nvmr-for-batteryless-computing/">
	Read the related Technical Perspective	<svg aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" width="24.88" height="16" fill="var(--wp--preset--color--cacm-link-blue)"><use href="#am-symbol-icon-arrow-right"></use></svg></a>

<div class="article-content entry-content">
		<article><div class="body" lang="en"><div style="background: #F5CBA7;"><h2>Abstract</h2><p>Batteryless devices powered by energy harvesting face frequent interruptions due to unstable power, requiring them to save their progress and resume later. These devices rely on non-volatile memories, such as Flash or FRAM, to preserve their state across power losses. However, saving data to these memories not only consumes significant energy, making frequent backups expensive, but can also lead to data inconsistencies and incorrect program execution when not handled appropriately.</p><p>This work introduces NvMR, an architecture that addresses these challenges by renaming non-volatile memory addresses to prevent inconsistencies. NvMR minimizes the need for backups, ensuring they occur only when absolutely necessary. It also simplifies the backup process, freeing it from program-specific constraints, and reduces energy consumption by 20%–40% compared to current state-of-the-art. These improvements make NvMR a significant step forward for energy-efficient and reliable operation in batteryless devices.</p></div><section id="sec1" class="sec"><h2 class="heading">1. Introduction</h2><p id="p-1">By 2030, the proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) devices is expected to reach staggering levels, with forecasts estimating more than 29 billion IoT-connected devices globally, growing at a compound annual rate of 13% from 2024.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B21" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B21"><sup>21</sup></a> This growth is driven by advances in connectivity technologies, such as 5G, low-energy Bluetooth, and sophisticated applications like machine learning, alongside expanding use cases in industrial automation, healthcare, smart homes, and urban infrastructure. A majority fraction of these devices relies on batteries, which are less eco-friendly because they wear out quickly due to frequent recharging. They are also inconvenient to replace in remote deployments, driving interest in <i>batteryless</i> devices that harvest energy from ambient sources, such as solar, RF, wind, or piezoelectric systems.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B6" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B6"><sup>6</sup></a><sup>,</sup><a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B8" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B8"><sup>8</sup></a><sup>,</sup><a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B15" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B15"><sup>15</sup></a><sup>,</sup><a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B18" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B18"><sup>18</sup></a></p><p id="p-2">Batteryless devices store energy in supercapacitors (higher recharge cycles but less dense energy storage) but face challenges due to unpredictable power supply. To ensure forward progress, they rely on <i>intermittent computing (IC)</i>, where the system backs up application and processor states to non-volatile memory (NVM) before energy depletes completely. Once recharged, the system restores state and resumes computation. The constraints of an IC system pose unique challenges that are normally not encountered in a continuously powered system.</p><section id="sec2" class="sec"><h3 class="heading"><span class="caption-label">1.1. </span>Challenges in IC.</h3><section id="sec3" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Inconsistency due to idempotency violations.</strong>  Unexpected power losses during program execution in batteryless devices can cause an incorrect processor state. To illustrate, in <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F1" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F1">Figure 1</a> we show the execution of a loop with the iterator stored at NVM location <code class="monospace">A</code>, the instruction <code class="monospace">LD A</code> initially reads a value of 0 from address <code class="monospace">A</code> and stores it in register <code class="monospace">r1</code>. <code class="monospace">ADDI</code> then increments the value in <code class="monospace">r1</code> by 1, and <code class="monospace">ST A</code> writes the value of <code class="monospace">r1</code> back to address <code class="monospace">A</code> in NVM. However, if a power outage occurs after the store, re-executing the program causes <code class="monospace">LD A</code> to read the updated value of 1 from NVM and the subsequent <code class="monospace">ST A</code> storing a value of 2, resulting in skipping an iteration, which can ultimately lead to incorrect behavior. This inconsistency in the result of <code class="monospace">LD A</code> from the result prior to power failure is referred to as an <i>idempotency violation</i>, an artifact of write-after-read (WAR) dependency between <code class="monospace">LD A</code> and <code class="monospace">ST A</code>.</p><figure id="F1" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig01.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 1. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig01.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 1. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig01.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F1" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 1. </span> <span class="p">A sudden power loss can cause an idempotency violation in intermittent program execution.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure></section><section id="sec4" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Expensive conservative backups.</strong>  IC systems require backups to ensure data consistency during power failures. Backup schemes can be broadly classified as hardware- or software-based. Hardware-based approaches<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B2" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B2"><sup>2</sup></a><sup>,</sup><a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B10" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B10"><sup>10</sup></a> use an analog-to-digital converter (ADC) to constantly monitor supply voltage and trigger a backup just before power failure. To ensure correctness, backups must be atomic (<a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F2" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F2">Figure 2a</a>). Software-based methods<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B5" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B5"><sup>5</sup></a><sup>,</sup><a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B12" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B12"><sup>12</sup></a> decompose programs into atomic tasks, with backups at task boundaries (<a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F2" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F2">Figure 2c</a>). While these approaches ensure consistency, they often impose unnecessary backups due to conservative policies or static task decomposition.</p><figure id="F2" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig02.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 2. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig02.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 2. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig02.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F2" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 2. </span> <span class="p">(a) Backup policy with an optimistic threshold (backup must be atomic), (b) Clank (backup when there is an idempotency violation), (c) DINO (backup at the boundary of predefined atomic tasks), (d) NvMR (any backup policy of choice); <code class="monospace">R</code> stands for renaming. NVM addresses <code class="monospace">A</code> and <code class="monospace">B</code> are renamed to <code class="monospace">X</code> and <code class="monospace">Y</code>.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><p id="p-7">Clank<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B9" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B9"><sup>9</sup></a> and DINO<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B12" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B12"><sup>12</sup></a> highlight the trade-offs in idempotency handling. Clank uses buffers to classify addresses as read-first or write-first, triggering backups on idempotency violations caused by store operations (<a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F2" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F2">Figure 2b</a>). Although effective, this approach results in frequent backups, even when sufficient energy is available. Our experiments with Clank show that such violations account for more than 30% of total energy consumption in common embedded benchmarks. DINO, on the other hand, enforces task-level idempotency with privatization buffers, but static task decomposition limits flexibility and increases programmer effort. The challenge is to balance backups for correctness with forward progress. An ideal solution would ensure program correctness while optimizing energy efficiency.</p><p id="p-8">We propose <i>NvMR</i>, an IC architecture that overcomes these challenges by dynamically renaming NVM addresses of violating persist stores (<a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F2" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F2">Figure 2d</a>), decoupling backup policies from program characteristics, and allowing energy-efficient backup policies under varied conditions.</p></section></section></section><section id="sec5" class="sec"><h2 class="heading"><span class="caption-label">2. </span>Intermittent Persist Dependencies</h2><p id="p-9">Most of the previous works have built models to characterize the efficiency of IC, but none provide generalized models addressing the persistency constraints required for correct execution under intermittent power. Persist dependencies, essential for ensuring programs behave as if running on a continuously powered system, remain unexplored. While memory-persistency models<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B17" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B17"><sup>17</sup></a> in shared-memory multiprocessors address orderings between persists to an external persistent data structure, this work introduces the first persist dependency model tailored for addressing the persist orderings imposed by IC.</p><p id="p-10">Previous studies proposing correctness models for IC assume architectures with no dirty data buffering, such as write-through caches.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B23" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B23"><sup>23</sup></a> In contrast, we consider an architecture with a volatile write-back cache, which reduces costly NVM writes by reusing dirty data. Thus, there is a good chance that the persists for backups and stores can get reordered. Execution resumes from the last persisted backup after power loss. Any instructions executed after the backup must be re-executed. This work aims to provide a theoretical framework for understanding persist dependencies and their impact on intermittent execution.</p><section id="sec6" class="inline-headings-section"><h3 data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>2.1. Requirements for correct IC.</strong> </h3><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading">Correct execution in traditional intermittent architectures relies on three key requirements:</p><ol class="list" data-jats-list-type="order"><li class="list-item"><p id="p-12"><i>Code Progress</i>. Stores to the same address must be persisted in program order, and backups must be persisted in the order they are triggered to ensure the program completes if the device sustains energy for at least one backup.</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-13"><i>Data Progress</i>. A store to NVM address <code class="monospace">X</code> must be persisted before the backup following it, ensuring data remains up to date across power losses.</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-14"><i>Idempotency</i>. After a backup, if the first access to an address <code class="monospace">X</code> is a load, subsequent stores to <code class="monospace">X</code> must not be persisted until the next backup. This prevents data corruption from unbacked instructions after a power loss.</p></li></ol></section><section id="sec7" class="inline-headings-section"><h3 data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>2.2. Persist ordering model.</strong>  </h3><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading">To ensure correct execution in intermittent systems, it is essential to define the order of persist operations, including backups and stores. Current systems often enforce overly conservative constraints, limiting efficiency. We aim to characterize the <i>minimum persist dependencies</i> required for correctness, balancing flexibility and reliability. Specifically, <i>given a program and a set of backups invoked during its execution, what are the minimum ordering constraints for correct persists?</i> Each backup captures the volatile processor state, including registers and the program counter, and persists it into NVM. Similar to stores, persisting the backup can be decoupled from its execution as long as the persisted register contents match what they would be at the position in program order at which the backup was triggered. The processor may trigger a backup and continue executing instructions after it; however, if power is lost before the backup is persisted, the processor must revert its state to an earlier backup. A backup must be persisted atomically via double buffering.</p><p id="p-16">We formally describe persist dependencies using <i>happensbefore</i> relations between persist operations, expressed as {<i>p1,rel,p2</i>}:</p><ul class="list" data-jats-list-type="bullet"><li class="list-item"><p id="p-17"><i>p1</i> and <i>p2</i> are persist operations in program order (e.g., store or checkpoint).</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-18"><i>rel</i> represents the persist order between a pair of persists with the order direction.</p></li></ul><p id="p-19">For example, <span id="IEq1" class="inline-formula"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="false"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></math></span> is the relation between a pair of persist stores to the same address, representing the necessary ordering between them to maintain program order (Requirement 1). We color-code the various relationships that exist in an intermittent execution and list them alongside the requirements they satisfy in <a class="xref xref-table" href="#T1" data-jats-ref-type="table" data-jats-rid="T1">Table 1</a>. The ordering, <span id="IEq2" class="inline-formula"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="false"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></math></span>, is also needed for correct program execution in continuously powered systems, while the other orderings are specific to IC systems. <span id="IEq3" class="inline-formula"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="false"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi></math></span>ordering is necessary in multi-backup schemes only.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B20" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B20"><sup>20</sup></a></p><figure id="T1" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><figcaption><span class="caption-label">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Table1-RH-3840403_fig15.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="" href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Table1-RH-3840403_fig15.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-812655" src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Table1-RH-3840403_fig15.jpg" alt="Table 1 RH" width="1000" height="555" srcset="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Table1-RH-3840403_fig15.jpg 1000w, https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Table1-RH-3840403_fig15.jpg?resize=300,167 300w, https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Table1-RH-3840403_fig15.jpg?resize=768,426 768w, https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Table1-RH-3840403_fig15.jpg?resize=620,344 620w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px" />
			</a>
		<br />Table 1. </span> <span class="p">Persist happens-before orderings (<i>spo</i>: store persist order, <i>bpo</i>: backup persist order, <i>rfpo</i>: read from persist order, and <i>irpo</i>: idempotent re-execution enforced persist order) in an intermittent execution, along with the correctness requirements they fulfill, shown with color-coded arrows.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><p id="p-21"><a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F3" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F3">Figure 3</a> shows the necessary persist dependencies for any general program execution, characterized by read-dominance and write-dominance, focusing on the symbolic NVM location <code class="monospace">X</code>. We ignore the dependencies in instruction execution and assume they respect program order. We also ignore loads and assume the most up-to-date values are readily available in the write-back cache, except immediately after a power loss; in that case, a load must fetch the value from NVM. Backups can be invoked at any arbitrary point in the program, but when invoked, they introduce a set of happensbefore orderings and atomicity constraints with surrounding stores.</p><figure id="F3" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig03.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 3. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig03.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 3. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig03.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F3" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 3. </span> <span class="p">Happens-before persist orderings for correct intermittent execution. Cyclic sequence of orderings represents atomicity constraints.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><section id="sec8" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Understanding persist orderings in IC.</strong>  The backup point also decides if an address location <code class="monospace">X</code> is read-dominated or write-dominated in the next intermittent code section.</p><p id="p-24"><i>Read-dominance</i>. A location <code class="monospace">X</code> is read-dominated if the first load after the most recent checkpoint occurs before the first store in program order. <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F3" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F3">Figure 3(a)</a> models the dependency pattern when all stores access read-dominated addresses. To ensure data progress (Requirement 2), each store must persist before the next backup persists. Otherwise, if the next backup were persisted first and power is lost, the most up-to-date value of <code class="monospace">X</code> would be lost. Simultaneously, to ensure idempotent execution (Requirement 3), each store must not persist until the next backup persists. Otherwise, if the store were persisted first and power is lost, the program would re-execute from the prior backup and incorrectly load the later store’s value of <code class="monospace">X</code>. These constraints combined imply that all stores to a read-dominated address must atomically persist with the next backup. This is a unique, fundamental challenge in IC systems.</p><p id="p-25"><i>Write-dominance</i>. A location <code class="monospace">X</code> is write-dominated if the first store occurs before the first load. <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F3" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F3">Figure 3(b)</a> shows that for the same program, with a simple shift of when backups are invoked, <code class="monospace">X</code> becomes write-dominated. As a result, the dependency pattern changes dramatically. Now only Requirements 1 and 2 need to be enforced, which is done by persisting backups in program order and ensuring stores are persisted before the next backup. Stores no longer must wait until the next backup to persist, eliminating the atomicity constraint. In fact, stores after a backup may even be persisted before the backup persists. Since <code class="monospace">X</code> is always write-dominated, whenever power is lost, regardless to which backup system it reverts, <code class="monospace">X</code> will always be overwritten first, nullifying the last persisted store.</p></section><section id="sec9" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Why non-volatile memory renaming?</strong>  Idempotency is essential for correct intermittent execution and can only be violated by WAR dependencies on NVM locations. Our architecture addresses this by dynamically <i>renaming</i> read-dominated NVM addresses, inspired by similar techniques used in out-of-order processors for registers. <i>NVM renaming</i> ensures idempotent execution (Requirement 3) regardless of backup timing.</p><p id="p-27">The key insight is: <i>renaming transforms all NVM addresses into write-dominated ones.</i> As shown in <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F4" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F4">Figure 4</a>, renaming eliminates dependencies such as <span id="IEq4" class="inline-formula"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="false"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></span>, <span id="IEq5" class="inline-formula"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="false"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></span>, and <span id="IEq6" class="inline-formula"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline"><mfenced open="{" close="}"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="false"><mo>←</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>o</mi></mrow></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>u</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></span>  from <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F3" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F3">Figure 3(a)</a>.</p><figure id="F4" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig04.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 4. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig04.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 4. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig04.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F4" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 4. </span> <span class="p">Happens-before persist orderings with proposed NVM renaming. By renaming <code class="monospace">X</code>, all stores access only write-dominated memory locations. Only the stores that immediately precede backups must be persisted.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><p id="p-29">This approach achieves the theoretical maximum efficiency by only persisting stores immediately preceding a backup, satisfying Requirement 2 (data progress). Backups must still be persisted in the order invoked to ensure Requirement 1 (code progress). Thus, renaming minimizes persist constraints, allowing backup decisions to be decoupled from program correctness requirements.</p></section></section></section><section id="sec10" class="sec"><h2 class="heading"><span class="caption-label">3. </span>NvMR Architecture</h2><p id="p-30">Based on insights from our previous analysis, we propose an intermittent architecture, <i>non-volatile memory renaming</i> (<i>NvMR</i>), that efficiently detects idempotency violations due to persist stores in hardware and renames their NVM addresses. <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F5" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F5">Figure 5</a> is a high-level block diagram of NvMR. <i>Map table</i> and <i>free list</i> are two software data structures allocated statically in NVM at compile time. Map table keeps a record of the old (original or most recently persisted) mappings. Free list is a queue of available mappings. Four hardware components are important for NvMR’s renaming mechanism:</p><ol class="list" data-jats-list-type="order"><li class="list-item"><p id="p-31">Write-back, write-allocate (WBWA) data cache that exploits data locality and tracks dirty blocks</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-32">Global bloom filter (GBF) that logs the composite state (Unknown=0 or Read-dominated=1; discussed later) of a block when it is evicted from the cache</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-33">Local bloom filter (LBF) array, tightly coupled with cache data array, with one LBF per block that logs the state of each word in a block (Unknown=00, Read-dominated=01, and Write-dominated=10)</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-34">Map table cache, a volatile cache for map table (analogous to a translation lookaside buffer for page table) that saves energy and time by reducing the number of trips to NVM. Apart from the old mapping, it also holds the new (latest) mapping. Since it is also a WBWA cache, for each block there is a dirty bit along with a valid bit and a tag.</p></li></ol><figure id="F5" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig05.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 5. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig05.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 5. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig05.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F5" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 5. </span> <span class="p">Block diagram of NvMR architecture (blocks shown in gold are added.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><section id="sec11" class="inline-headings-section"><h3 data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>3.1. Intermittent execution in NvMR.</strong> </h3><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F6" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F6">Figure 6</a> explains NvMR’s renaming mechanism in a concise flowchart. To explain NvMR’s microarchitecture in more detail, we divide intermittent execution in NvMR into five disparate events and explain them with a running example.</p><figure id="F6" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig06.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 6. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig06.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 6. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig06.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F6" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 6. </span> <span class="p">Flowchart showing the handling of idempotency violations in NvMR.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><section id="sec12" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Idempotency violation.</strong>  <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F7" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F7">Figure 7</a> shows a cache block <code class="monospace">X</code> with a tag, <code class="monospace">X</code><code class="monospace"><sub>tag</sub></code>. This block was fetched from NVM because of a read-dominated access to word [<code class="monospace">C</code>]. Later, as word [<code class="monospace">A</code>] is updated to [<code class="monospace">A</code><code class="monospace"><sub>new</sub></code>], the block is marked dirty and the corresponding LBF states of [<code class="monospace">C</code>] and [<code class="monospace">A</code><code class="monospace"><sub>new</sub></code>] are set as <code class="monospace">R</code> and <code class="monospace">W</code> respectively. Only when a dirty block is evicted is the composite state of the block calculated by OR of the LSBs of the LBF states of the constituent words. If the composite state is 0 (block is write-dominated), there is no idempotency violation. Otherwise, it should be flagged as an idempotency violation since it is dirty and read-dominated (violates Requirement 3). Thus, NvMR flags an idempotency violation (A1) when block <code class="monospace">X</code> is evicted. The composite state is logged in GBF.</p><figure id="F7" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig07.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 7. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig07.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 7. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig07.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F7" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 7. </span> <span class="p">Eviction of a cache block with tag <code class="monospace">X<sub>tag</sub></code> causes an idempotency violation. Cache block address 200 is renamed to address 710. The dirty cache block is stored at the new mapping 710 instead of 200.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure></section><section id="sec13" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Renaming.</strong>  Eviction of an idempotency violating block triggers a lookup in the map table cache to find its new mapping. In case of a hit, the new mapping is used to persist the block. In case of a miss, map table is looked up. If the map table lookup is successful, the old mapping is fetched into the map table cache. Otherwise, a new mapping is popped from the head of the free list and fetched into the map table cache. The block is persisted using the fetched mapping. Thus, the old mapping of the persist store is renamed to a new mapping. In <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F7" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F7">Figure 7</a>, block <code class="monospace">X</code> incurs a miss in both map table cache (A2) and map table (A3). Thus, NvMR fetches mapping 710 from free list (A4) and inserts a new dirty map table cache block (A5), renaming 200 to 710. The contents of block <code class="monospace">X</code> are written to the renamed address 710 (A6).</p></section><section id="sec14" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Cache miss.</strong>  In <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F8" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F8">Figure 8</a>, a store request for cache block <code class="monospace">X</code> attempts to write [<code class="monospace">B</code><code class="monospace"><sub>new</sub></code>]. Since the block was recently evicted, the lookup results in a cache miss (B1). The cache controller then looks up the map table cache to find the latest mapping in NVM. As address 200 was renamed to 710 and this entry exists in the map table cache, the lookup is a hit (B2). The block is fetched from 710 in NVM, updated with [<code class="monospace">B</code><code class="monospace"><sub>new</sub></code>], and marked dirty (B3). NvMR then checks GBF to determine the block state. Since block <code class="monospace">X</code> is read-dominated (1), all associated LBF states are set to <code class="monospace">R</code> (B4). For blocks in the <code class="monospace">U</code> state, LBF states are marked <code class="monospace">R</code> or <code class="monospace">W</code> based on the type of request. A map table cache miss triggers a lookup in the map table, which lies in the critical path. We assume the map table lookup can be done in parallel to fetching the application data, since both reside in separate regions of NVM.</p><figure id="F8" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig08.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 8. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig08.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 8. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig08.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F8" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 8. </span> <span class="p">A store miss causes the cache block to be fetched from the new mapping 710. NvMR keeps a log of the new mapping in the map table cache.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure></section><section id="sec15" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Backup.</strong>  NvMR initiates a backup in three cases: (1) when a dirty map table cache entry is evicted to ensure the map table in NVM reflects the latest mappings, (2) based on a user-defined backup policy to guarantee forward progress, and (3) when an idempotency violation occurs and either the map table is full or the free list is empty, making renaming impossible. Like other intermittent architectures, NvMR snapshots general-purpose and special registers (e.g., the program counter), but these are omitted in <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F9" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F9">Figure 9</a>. With a write-back data cache, NvMR persists all dirty blocks during backup. If a cache block was renamed, NvMR fetches its latest mapping from the map table cache or the map table; otherwise, the block is stored at its program address.</p><figure id="F9" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig09.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 9. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig09.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 9. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig09.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F9" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 9. </span> <span class="p">Backup will cause the data cache to persist its dirty blocks. The old mappings from the map table cache are pushed into the free list, and the new mappings are written to the map table. All states in LBF and GBF are reset.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><p id="p-45">For cache block <code class="monospace">X</code>, the map table cache lookup is a hit (C1), and the dirty data is persisted at the new mapping 710 (C2). The dirty bit of block <code class="monospace">X</code> is cleared after the write-back. NvMR writes the new mapping 710 to the map table (C3) and appends the old mapping 200 to the free list tail (C4), indicated by the orange arrow for the write pointer. Once all updates are completed, the new mapping is copied to the old field in the map table cache, and the dirty bit is reset. Finally, GBF and LBF arrays are cleared (C5).</p></section><section id="sec16" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Restore.</strong>  After a power loss, an IC system state is restored to the most recent backup and execution resumes. In NvMR, restore is pretty simple: The program counter and other registers are restored from the most recent snapshot saved in NVM. Moreover, only the read and write pointers of the free list need to be restored.</p></section><section id="sec17" class="inline-headings-section"><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Reclamation.</strong>  The map table, being a limited hardware resource, can fill quickly for applications with frequent idempotency violations, especially in energy-harvesting devices with small non-volatile memory. To address this, we introduce reclamation (a popular technique in garbage collection), which reuses the original mapping—the program’s cache block address. When the map table is full after a backup, NvMR reclaims an entry using a cache-like replacement policy. This involves atomically moving the old mapping to the free list’s tail, invalidating the entry, and copying data from the freed mapping back to the original mapping. NvMR also invalidates the corresponding map table cache entry on a lookup hit. Reclaiming reduces backups after a full map table but decreases the free list’s effective size, as original mappings cannot be added. To compensate, the free list requires more mappings to avoid shortages.</p></section></section></section><section id="sec18" class="sec"><h2 class="heading"><span class="caption-label">4. </span>Methodology</h2><p id="p-48">We extend a cycle-accurate C++ simulator<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B20" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B20"><sup>20</sup></a> to model an intermittent ARM Cortex M0+<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B1" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B1"><sup>1</sup></a> (3-stage in-order pipeline) executing ARM Thumb ISA instructions. Voltage traces sampled at 1kHz from prior work<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B14" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B14"><sup>14</sup></a> are used to compute the charge across the supply supercapacitor. Dynamic and leakage power for added volatile components are modeled using CACTI,<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B16" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B16"><sup>16</sup></a> and NVM energy values are taken from the STM32L011K4 MCU data sheet.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B22" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B22"><sup>22</sup></a></p><p id="p-49">We compare NvMR with two IC architectures: a) a modified <i>Clank</i><a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B9" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B9"><sup>9</sup></a>, augmented with local and global bloom filters, and a write-back data cache, an idempotency violation-aware hardware solution, and b) <i>HOOP</i><a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B4" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B4"><sup>4</sup></a>, a software transactional memory solution with hardware support with similar semantics to a task-based IC architecture. We use the configuration listed in <a class="xref xref-table" href="#T2" data-jats-ref-type="table" data-jats-rid="T2">Table 2</a> for all our results except for the sensitivity analysis because it is pareto-optimal. Since NvMR decouples the backup policy from the memory access patterns of an application, we show the impact of NvMR using three backup schemes:</p><ul class="list" data-jats-list-type="bullet"><li class="list-item"><p id="p-50"><i>Just-In-Time</i> (JIT) is an oracle that accurately estimates when a power loss will happen and triggers a backup just before it.</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-51"><i>Spendthrift</i> uses a lightweight neural network (trained offline with two inputs, available ambient energy, and supercapacitor voltages at a particular time to an accuracy of 97%) to predict backups,<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B13" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B13"><sup>13</sup></a> representative of JIT schemes deployed commercially.</p></li><li class="list-item"><p id="p-52"><i>Watchdog timer</i> is the most naive out of the three and invokes a backup every <i>8,000</i> cycles.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B9" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B9"><sup>9</sup></a></p></li></ul><figure id="T2" class="table-wrap" data-jats-position="float"><div class="caption"><span class="caption-label">Table 2. </span> <span class="p">System configuration used in evaluation.</span></div><div class="table-container"><table class="table table-bordered table-condensed table-hover" data-jats-frame="hsides" data-jats-rules="groups"><thead style="vertical-align: bottom;"><tr><th style="text-align: left;">Parameter</th><th style="text-align: left;">Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align: left;">Processor</td><td style="text-align: left;">ARM Cortex M0+ (Thumb ISA), 8MHz</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align: left;">Data Cache</td><td style="text-align: left;">256B, 8-way associative, 16B block, LRU, 1-cycle hit latency</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align: left;">GBF</td><td style="text-align: left;">8 one-bit entries</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align: left;">LBF</td><td style="text-align: left;">4 two-bit entries per cache line</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align: left;">Map Table Cache</td><td style="text-align: left;">512 entries, 8-way associative, LRU</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align: left;">Map Table</td><td style="text-align: left;">4,096 entries, LRU</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align: left;">Free List</td><td style="text-align: left;">4,096 + 512 + 1 = 4,609 mappings</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align: left;">Flash</td><td style="text-align: left;">2MB</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align: left;">Supercapacitor</td><td style="text-align: left;">100mF, 2.4V max. voltage</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure><p id="p-54">The benchmarks we chose are from MiBench<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B7" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B7"><sup>7</sup></a> and PERFECT<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B3" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B3"><sup>3</sup></a> benchmark suites, representing applications typically found in ultra-low-power IoT systems with a high number of idempotency violations.</p></section><section id="sec19" class="sec"><h2 class="heading"><span class="caption-label">5. </span>Evaluating NvMR</h2><p id="p-55">To understand the benefits offered by NvMR, we compare it with the chosen baseline IC architectures by looking at the energy savings in each architecture with different backup schemes. In addition, we analyze how sensitive NvMR is to variations in system configuration. Further, we discuss the minimal overheads incurred with NvMR. All results are averaged over 10 voltage traces.</p><section id="sec20" class="inline-headings-section"><h3 data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>5.1. NvMR vs. Clank.</strong> </h3><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F10" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F10">Figure 10</a> presents the percentage of energy saved in NvMR compared to Clank. With JIT, NvMR saves on average about 20% energy compared to Clank. The energy savings can range from 2% to 37% based on the application. With spendthrift, NvMR saves about 15.6% energy on average. Since <i>Blowfish</i> and <i>dijkstra</i> spend the least amount of energy on backups due to idempotency violations, they marginally consume more energy in NvMR. With watchdog timer, NvMR saves on average 9% energy on average. <i>stringsearch</i> and <i>hist</i> perform worse than in Clank because a significant portion of energy (6.5%–7%) is wasted in re-executing instructions after a power failure (dead energy<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B20" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B20"><sup>20</sup></a>).</p><figure id="F10" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig10.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 10. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig10.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 10. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig10.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F10" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 10. </span> <span class="p">Energy saved in NvMR with different backup schemes compared to Clank.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><p id="p-58">We take a deeper dive by analyzing the breakdown of normalized energy consumption of Clank and NvMR with JIT backup scheme in <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F11" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F11">Figure 11</a>. The total energy consumption is split into four components—<i>forward progress, backup, restore,</i> and <i>dead</i> energy.<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B20" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B20"><sup>20</sup></a> NvMR incurs additional energy overhead for forward progress and restore due to dynamic and leakage energy from the map table cache, extra NVM accesses for the map table and free list, and reclamation-related energy.</p><figure id="F11" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig11.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 11. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig11.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 11. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig11.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F11" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 11. </span> <span class="p">Breakdown of normalized energy consumption of Clank (left bars) and NvMR (right bars).</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><p id="p-60">JIT backup scheme eliminates dead energy, and NvMR’s restore energy is minimal, involving only the retrieval of CPU registers; hence, these are omitted from <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F11" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F11">Figure 11</a>. The benchmark <i>stringsearch</i> performs worst, with nearly 90% of its energy spent on forward progress due to minimal idempotency violations during string list searches. NvMR incurs forward progress and backup overheads, along with additional energy for reclaiming. Forward progress energy is higher in NvMR than Clank for some benchmarks, since persisting data to renamed addresses is included in forward progress rather than backup energy. Reclaim energy is significant only for benchmarks such as <i>blowfish</i>, <i>dijkstra</i>, and <i>qsort</i>, where frequent idempotency violations fill the map table. Overall, renaming and reclaiming add just 3% to NvMR’s total energy, a small cost for reducing backups by 185x on average.</p></section><section id="sec21" class="inline-headings-section"><h3 data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>5.2. NvMR vs. Hoop.</strong> </h3><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading">HOOP, a log-based architecture, performs out-of-place updates to an NVM region, optimizing write efficiency and triggering backups when its buffer or mapping table fills up. <a class="xref xref-table" href="#T3" data-jats-ref-type="table" data-jats-rid="T3">Table 3</a> outlines the HOOP configuration used for the results in <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F12" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F12">Figure 12</a>. Components, excluding the mapping table (idealized as infinite for no energy usage), are sized to match NvMR’s on-chip area and memory overheads (<a class="xref xref-table" href="#T2" data-jats-ref-type="table" data-jats-rid="T2">Table 2</a>). With JIT, NvMR saves 40% more energy on average, except for benchmarks <i>stringsearch, picojpeg</i>, and <i>basicmath</i>, where HOOP excels due to high store locality, allowing its buffer to reduce NVM writes. For watchdog timer backup, NvMR achieves 19.4% average energy savings, although less than with JIT due to the naive nature of the timer scheme. (Spendthrift results are not shown since they were not part of the original paper.)</p><figure id="T3" class="table-wrap" data-jats-position="float"><div class="caption"><span class="caption-label">Table 3. </span> <span class="p">System configuration of a simplified version of HOOP. The infinitely large map table has no energy and area overhead.</span></div><div class="table-container"><table class="table table-bordered table-condensed table-hover" data-jats-frame="hsides" data-jats-rules="groups"><thead style="vertical-align: bottom;"><tr><th style="text-align: left;">Structure</th><th style="text-align: left;">Mapping Table</th><th style="text-align: left;">OOP Buffer</th><th style="text-align: left;">OOP Region</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align: left;">Size</td><td style="text-align: left;">Infinite</td><td style="text-align: left;">128</td><td style="text-align: left;">2048</td></tr></tbody></table></div></figure><figure id="F12" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig12.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 12. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig12.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 12. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig12.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F12" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 12. </span> <span class="p">Energy saved in NvMR compared to HOOP.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure></section><section id="sec22" class="inline-headings-section"><h3 data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>5.3. NvMR sensitivity.</strong> </h3><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>Map Table Cache.</strong>  Energy savings in NvMR improve with larger map table caches, as seen in <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F13" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F13">Figure 13b</a>, since evictions of dirty entries (and the associated backups) reduce. Associativity has minimal impact on energy efficiency after reaching four-way; savings plateau at around 0.2% with full associativity, as shown in <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F13" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F13">Figure 13a</a>. These results use a map table with 4,096 entries and JIT backup for consistency.</p><figure id="F13" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig13.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 13. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig13.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 13. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig13.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F13" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 13. </span> <span class="p">Energy saved with different map table cache and map table configurations and capacitor sizes.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure><p id="p-66"><strong>Map Table Size.</strong>  Increasing the map table entries from 1,024 to 8,192 shows a modest 1% energy saving (<a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F13" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F13">Figure 13c</a>), primarily benefiting benchmarks prone to idempotency violations, such as <i>blowfish, dijkstra, qsort</i>, and <i>dwt</i>. Given the large Flash requirements for small gains, 4,096 entries represents a balanced choice, aligning with the constraints of energy-harvesting devices.</p><p id="p-67"><strong>Capacitor Size.</strong>  NvMR’s energy savings grow as capacitor size increases, with a 7% improvement from 500µF to 7.5mF and a slower 2% rise up to 100mF (<a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F13" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F13">Figure 13d</a>). Larger capacitors extend active periods, leading to more idempotency violations, which NvMR exploits to reduce backups. However, the rate of savings diminishes beyond 7.5mF, reflecting diminishing returns on larger capacitor sizes.</p></section><section id="sec23" class="inline-headings-section"><h3 data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>5.4. Benefits of reclamation.</strong> </h3><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading">Reclamation minimizes backups caused by idempotency violations when the map table is full, but only a few benchmarks encounter this issue with the configuration in <a class="xref xref-table" href="#T2" data-jats-ref-type="table" data-jats-rid="T2">Table 2</a>. As shown in <a class="xref xref-fig" href="#F14" data-jats-ref-type="fig" data-jats-rid="F14">Figure 14</a>, reclaiming provides minimal energy savings, averaging just 1%, without diminishing NvMR’s overall efficiency <i>qsort</i> (9%) and <i>dwt</i> (1%) benefit, while <i>dijkstra</i>, <i>blowfish</i>, and <i>2dconv</i> incur slightly higher energy consumption). Nonetheless, reclamation can be particularly valuable for devices with limited NVM, where allocating large map tables and free lists is impractical. Our experiments reveal with a map table of 1,024 entries, reclaiming saves about 9% more energy compared to the “no reclaim” scenario.</p><figure id="F14" class="fig" data-jats-position="float"><div class="image-container">
			<a data-fslightbox="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig14.jpg" data-type="image" data-caption="Figure 14. " href="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig14.jpg">
				<img decoding="async" class="graphic" title="Figure 14. " src="https://cacm.acm.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/3840403_fig14.jpg" alt="" data-image-id="F14" data-image-type="figure" />
			</a>
		</div><figcaption><span class="caption-label">Figure 14. </span> <span class="p">Energy saved in NvMR with and without reclaim.</span><div class="figcaption-footer"> </div></figcaption></figure></section><section id="sec24" class="inline-headings-section"><h3 data-jats-content-type="inline-heading"><strong>5.5. NvMR overhead.</strong> </h3><p data-jats-content-type="inline-heading">The hardware cost of NvMR is limited to the map table cache, with McPAT<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B11" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B11"><sup>11</sup></a> estimates indicating a modest 6% on-chip area overhead for the configuration in <a class="xref xref-table" href="#T2" data-jats-ref-type="table" data-jats-rid="T2">Table 2</a>. NvMR’s software and hardware structures can be tailored to application requirements, constrained only by the available memory onboard. The memory footprint for the reserved region is approximately 6% of the 2MB Flash (typical size of Flash in commercial MCU boards<a class="reference-link xref xref-bibr" href="#B19" data-jats-ref-type="bibr" data-jats-rid="B19"><sup>19</sup></a>). However, if the NVM is byte-addressable, NvMR’s renaming mechanism provides a significant advantage by reducing wear-out, achieving an impressive 80.8% average decrease in maximum writes per location compared to Clank.</p></section></section><section id="sec25" class="sec"><h2 class="heading">Conclusion</h2><p id="p-71">This work addresses a critical challenge in energy-harvesting intermittent systems: backups caused by idempotency violations. While prior research has improved backup efficiency, unnecessary overhead from these violations persists. Our key insight is that backups should stem solely from energy or hardware constraints, not program inconsistencies. By analyzing unique persist dependencies in intermittent execution, we show that renaming NVM addresses of violating persists minimizes backups. Based on this, we propose NvMR, an energy-efficient hardware architecture that enables productive use of harvested energy and decouples program correctness from backup policies, paving the way for advancements in intermittent system design.</p></section></div><footer class="back"></footer></article>
